---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfMetadataEditor from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfMetadataEditor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-metadata-editor">
  <PdfMetadataEditor client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>What a PDF knows about you</h2>
      <p>
        Open any PDF you did not make yourself and look at File → Properties. There is a decent
        chance it tells you the name of the person whose computer account created it, the exact
        software and version they used, and the minute they saved it. None of that is visible on the
        page, and almost nobody checks it before sending a document out.
      </p>
      <p>
        The classic failures are well documented: a tender submitted with a competitor&rsquo;s name
        still in the Author field because the template was reused; a &ldquo;newly drafted&rdquo;
        contract whose creation timestamp is eighteen months old; an anonymous complaint whose
        Creator field names the department&rsquo;s single licensed copy of a niche application.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The fields, and where they come from</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Field</th>
            <th scope="col">Typically set by</th>
            <th scope="col">What it can reveal</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Title</td>
            <td>The authoring application, often from the source filename</td>
            <td>Internal project names, draft numbers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Author</td>
            <td>The operating-system account name</td>
            <td>A real name, or a corporate username format</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Subject / Keywords</td>
            <td>Manually, or by a document management system</td>
            <td>Internal classification, matter numbers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Creator</td>
            <td>The program the document was written in</td>
            <td>&ldquo;Microsoft Word for Microsoft 365&rdquo;, &ldquo;LaTeX with hyperref&rdquo;</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Producer</td>
            <td>The library that wrote the PDF</td>
            <td>The exact toolchain and version</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CreationDate / ModDate</td>
            <td>Automatically, to the second, with a timezone offset</td>
            <td>When it was really written, and in which timezone</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Two metadata stores, not one</h2>
      <p>
        PDF carries metadata in two places. The <strong>information dictionary</strong> is the old
        key-value block listed above. The <strong>XMP packet</strong> is an XML document stored in
        the catalogue, added by most modern PDF writers, and it can hold considerably
        more: original filenames, an instance ID and a document ID that link successive revisions of
        the same file together, the software history, and sometimes embedded thumbnails.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most &ldquo;remove PDF metadata&rdquo; tools clear only the information dictionary. That leaves
        the XMP packet, and a text editor can still recover the same information. The panel above tells you whether an XMP block is present,
        and <strong>Strip everything</strong> removes both.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Clearing a field really clears it</h2>
      <p>
        There is a subtlety here that trips up a lot of libraries. Writing an empty string into the
        Author entry is not the same as deleting the entry: some readers show the empty value,
        others fall back to a cached copy, and a raw inspection still shows the key. When you empty
        a field in this editor, the key is deleted from the information dictionary outright, and
        cleared dates remove the <code>CreationDate</code> and <code>ModDate</code> entries rather
        than writing 1970.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What this tool does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          It does not remove metadata embedded <em>inside</em> images on the page. A photograph with
          GPS EXIF data placed into a PDF keeps that data. Extract it with
          <a href="/extract-images-from-pdf">Extract Images from PDF</a> and inspect it separately.
        </li>
        <li>
          It does not remove tracked changes, comments or hidden layers. Those are page content, not
          metadata.
        </li>
        <li>
          It does not remove text that is merely covered by a black rectangle. That is a redaction
          problem, and covering is not removing.
        </li>
        <li>
          It cannot preserve an existing password or permission set. A protected file can be read
          here, but the saved copy will be unprotected.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Reading metadata is as useful as writing it</h2>
      <p>
        Open the panel even when you do not want to change anything. The Producer field tells you what
        made a document, which helps you verify that a scan really
        came from the scanner it claims. Checking the dates on a contract, an invoice or a
        submission is a thirty-second sanity test that occasionally turns up something important.
      </p>
      <p>
        And because nothing is uploaded, you can run that check on a file you would never hand to a
        web service: the document is opened by pdf-lib in this tab and stays there.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
